= Introduction

Walk through an upland oak--hickory forest in the eastern United States today, and you are often walking through a woods quietly changing beneath its mature treetops. For centuries, periodic low-intensity surface fires kept the forest floor open enough for sun-loving oak seedlings to recruit. After decades of fire exclusion, however, shade-tolerant hardwoods such as red maple, American beech, and blackgum have expanded into the middle layer beneath the oaks. Because these trees cast deeper shade and drop damp, fast-rotting leaves, they cool and moisten the forest floor so it resists burning, a self-reinforcing shift ecologists call _mesophication_ @Nowacki2008 @Alexander2021.

Monitoring how this hidden middle layer changes across entire mountain regions is difficult. Field burn trials measure individual trees accurately @Arthur2015, yet crews cannot inventory every ridge across hundreds of kilometers. Seeking a regional view from four decades of Landsat satellite imagery is therefore tempting. #cite(<Chastain2007>, form: "prose") showed that leaf-off winter greenness (the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index, or NDVI) maps _evergreen_ rhododendron and mountain laurel beneath leafless Appalachian oaks. Extending that winter-NDVI recipe to _deciduous_ hardwood mesophication, as we initially attempted in an exploratory unharmonized workflow on Tennessee's Cumberland Plateau, produces a steep apparent upward trend that looks like rapid understory crowding.

A direct radiometric audit shows why that apparent trend fails. Comparing older and newer Landsat satellites over overlapping calendar years on identical intact hardwood pixels reveals a large winter NDVI step jump between TM and OLI sensors in Collection 2, more than twice the jump in summer. Standard Collection 1 adjustment formulas @Roy2016 remove only 7% of this regional winter slope. Moreover, once deciduous maples and beeches drop their leaves in autumn, bare woody stems emit no greenness signal. Passive winter reflectance therefore tracks sensor transitions, maturing pine plantations, and temporary burn char rather than hardwood understory growth.

We replace passive winter reflectance with three-dimensional leaf-off aircraft laser scanning (USGS airborne LiDAR). Because winter laser pulses pass through bare oak crowns and bounce off woody trunks and branches at every height, penetration-adjusted Normalized Relative Point Density (NRD) measures subcanopy crowding in discrete vertical slices (@fig-schematic). To avoid the mechanical upward bias that occurs when canopy cover is computed as a share of all returns, we measure the mature overstory with first-return canopy cover above ten meters. We assemble 689 intact, pure-deciduous upland forest plots across William B. Bankhead National Forest in Alabama, the Sewanee Domain and Franklin State Forest in Tennessee, and Daniel Boone National Forest in Kentucky (@fig-map), paired with official prescribed-fire perimeters and two multi-year repeat-LiDAR experiments.

Evaluating passive satellites, NASA GEDI, and airborne LiDAR on the exact same hardwood stands clarifies both what remote sensing can detect and how prescribed fire reshapes forest structure (@fig-optical). Empirical sensor calibration cuts the apparent regional winter trend by 79% and shifts seven of eight Tennessee protected areas slightly negative over the past decade. More directly, across paired repeat-scanned plots, *neither* winter ($r = -0.00$) *nor* early-spring ($r = -0.05$) Landsat NDVI tracks multi-year subcanopy change. Within sites, spring's cross-sectional correlation ($r = +0.30$) barely differs from winter NDVI or GEDI midstory plant area.

Repeat airborne LiDAR flights also differ in sensor hardware and multi-return sensitivity, so raw control trends are not identified on their own; causal inference therefore rests on burned-minus-unburned Difference-in-Differences (DiD) contrasts. In northern Bankhead National Forest, repeated inter-epoch burns produced large, density-invariant midstory reductions ($-0.066$ for one burn and $-0.076$ for two or more burns, burn-unit $p < 0.001$). By contrast, a single burn in steeper Daniel Boone National Forest produced no detectable six-year difference, and previously burned stands recovered from a lower starting baseline without exceeding unburned controls. Across all sampled plots, continuous one-meter vertical profiles show that repeated surface fires outperform mechanical thinning with fire below four meters, fade to statistical null above six meters, and leave unbiased first-return upper canopy cover unchanged. Cross-sectional mechanical thinning with fire is stronger near the top of the midstory while opening the overstory ($-0.067$). Together with our open analysis pipeline and interactive web explorer, these results provide a realistic regional benchmark for evaluating prescribed fire with public airborne LiDAR.
